    "professorName": "Dr. Halloran",
    "professorPost": "When a management position opens up, an organization can promote someone who already works there or recruit an experienced candidate from outside. Promoting from within rewards loyalty and draws on detailed knowledge of how the company actually operates, while hiring externally can bring in skills and perspectives the current staff does not have. Both choices carry costs. What do you think? When a management position opens up, should companies promote from within? Why or why not?",
